n class="enc-snip">The Herero Wars were the colonial wars in German South West Africa, now Namibia, in which the Herero and then the Nama rose against German rule in 1904.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herford"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herford.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herford</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Herford is a town in eastern Westphalia, in the German state of North Rhine-Westphalia, north-east of Bielefeld.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herge"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herge.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hergé</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hergé was the pen name of Georges Remi, a Belgian cartoonist best known for &quot;The Adventures of Tintin&quot;, which began in 1929.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herihor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herihor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herihor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herihor was an Egyptian army officer who became High Priest of Amun at Thebes around 1080 BCE, in the last years of Ramesses XI and the Twentieth Dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/herisau"><span class="enc-name">Herisau</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Herisau is a town in north-eastern Switzerland and the capital of the half-canton of Appenzell Ausserrhoden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/herius-asinius"><span class="enc-name">Herius Asinius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herius Asinius was a commander of the Marrucini, an Italic people of the Adriatic coast, during the Social War against Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herkus-monte"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herkus-monte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herkus Monte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herkus Monte was a leader of the Natangians, one of the Old Prussian peoples, who had been educated in Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/herluin"><span class="enc-name">Herluin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herluin was a Norman knight who gave up military life to become a monk and founded the abbey of Bec in Normandy around 1034, serving as its first abbot.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermaeus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermaeus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermaeus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermaeus was one of the last Indo-Greek kings, ruling in the Paropamisadae around the Kabul valley in the early 1st century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herman-boerhaave"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herman-boerhaave.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herman Boerhaave</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herman Boerhaave was a Dutch physician, chemist and botanist who taught at Leiden University from 1701.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herman Willem Daendels</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herman Willem Daendels was a Dutch Patriot and general who served as governor-general of the Dutch East Indies from 1808 to 1811 for Louis Bonaparte's Kingdom of Holland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-balk"><span class="enc-name">Hermann Balk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann Balk was a knight of the Teutonic Order who led its first campaigns in Prussia from 1230, founding Thorn, Kulm and Elbing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-billung"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-billung.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann Billung</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann Billung was a Saxon noble whom Otto I entrusted around 936 with command against the Slavs along the lower Elbe, the frontier later called the Billung March.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-goring.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann Göring</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann Göring was a leading Nazi, a First World War fighter pilot who became commander of the Luftwaffe and Hitler's designated successor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann Hesse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann Hesse was a German-born Swiss novelist and poet, born at Calw in Württemberg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-ii-margrave-of-baden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-ii-margrave-of-baden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann II, Margrave of Baden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann II was a member of the house of Zähringen and the first to call himself Margrave of Baden, in 1112, after the castle of Hohenbaden above Baden-Baden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-iv-margrave-of-baden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-iv-margrave-of-baden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann IV, Margrave of Baden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann IV was Margrave of Baden from 1160 until his death in 1190, a supporter of Emperor Frederick Barbarossa in his Italian wars.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-of-buxhoeveden"><span class="enc-name">Hermann of Buxhoeveden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann of Buxhoeveden was a German churchman, brother of Bishop Albert of Riga, who was made bishop of the Estonians in 1220.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-mittnacht"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-von-mittnacht.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann von Mittnacht</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann von Mittnacht was a Württemberg statesman who dominated the kingdom's government after German unification, serving as minister of justice from 1867 and as head…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-salza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-von-salza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermann von Salza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann von Salza was Grand Master of the Teutonic Order from 1210 to 1239.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-wissmann"><span class="enc-name">Hermann von Wissmann</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann von Wissmann was a German explorer and colonial officer who crossed Central Africa twice in the 1880s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-wesel"><span class="enc-name">Hermann Wesel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermann Wesel was the last Bishop of Dorpat, elected in 1554.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermanus-steyn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermanus-steyn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermanus Steyn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermanus Steyn was a burgher of the Swellendam district of the Dutch Cape Colony.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermanus-van-wyk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermanus-van-wyk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermanus van Wyk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermanus van Wyk was the first Kaptein of the Rehoboth Basters in present-day Namibia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermen-rode"><span class="enc-name">Hermen Rode</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermen Rode was a painter based in Lübeck, one of the leading artists of the Hanseatic world in the late 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermenegildo-gutierrez"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermenegildo-gutierrez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermenegildo Gutiérrez</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermenegildo Gutiérrez was a Galician noble in the service of Alfonso III of Asturias.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermeric"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermeric.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermeric</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermeric was king of the Suebi who led his people across the Rhine in 406 and into Hispania in 409.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermes</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermes was a Greek god, son of Zeus and Maia, the messenger of the gods and the guide of souls to the underworld.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermes-of-andros"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermes-of-andros.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermes of Andros</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hermes of Andros is a marble statue of the god Hermes as guide of souls, found in 1832 at Palaiopolis, the ancient city of Andros, in the Cyclades.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermigar"><span class="enc-name">Hermigar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermigar was a king of the Suebi in Hispania in the early 5th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid"><span class="enc-name">Herminafrid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herminafrid was the last king of the Thuringians, who married Amalaberga, niece of Theodoric the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermogenes-architect"><span class="enc-name">Hermogenes (architect)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermogenes was a Greek architect of the Hellenistic period, probably from Priene or Alabanda, admired by Vitruvius as an innovator in the Ionic order.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermonassa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermonassa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermonassa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermonassa was an ancient Greek city on the Taman peninsula, on the Asian side of the Cimmerian Bosporus, founded in the 6th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hermopolis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hermopolis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hermopolis, ancient Egyptian Khemenu and modern el-Ashmunein, was a city in Middle Egypt and the chief cult centre of Thoth, god of wisdom and writing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hernán Cortés</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hernán Cortés was a Spanish conquistador who led the expedition that brought down the Aztec Empire between 1519 and 1521.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-siles-zuazo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-siles-zuazo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hernán Siles Zuazo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hernán Siles Zuazo was a Bolivian politician, a founder of the Revolutionary Nationalist Movement (MNR) and a leader of the revolution of 1952.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernandarias"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hernandarias.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hernandarias</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hernandarias, Hernando Arias de Saavedra, was born in Asunción and was the first American-born governor of the Río de la Plata, holding office several times between…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hernando de Soto</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hernando de Soto was a Spanish conquistador who took part in the conquest of Peru and in 1539 led a large expedition into the south-eastern interior of North America.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hernando Pizarro</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hernando Pizarro was a Spanish conquistador, the only legitimate brother of Francisco Pizarro, and a leader in the conquest of the Inca Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hero-and-leander"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hero-and-leander.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hero and Leander</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Hero and Leander is a Greek myth about two lovers on either side of the Hellespont.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hero-of-alexandria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hero-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hero of Alexandria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hero of Alexandria was a Greek mathematician and engineer who worked in Alexandria in the first century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herod Agrippa I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herod Agrippa I was a grandson of Herod the Great, raised in Rome and a friend of the emperors Caligula and Claudius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-antipas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herod Antipas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herod Antipas, a son of Herod the Great, ruled Galilee and Peraea as tetrarch from 4 BCE to 39 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herod Archelaus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herod Archelaus was a son of Herod the Great who ruled Judea, Samaria and Idumaea as ethnarch from 4 BCE to 6 CE, after Augustus denied him the royal title.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herod of Chalcis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herod of Chalcis was a grandson of Herod the Great and brother of Agrippa I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herod the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herod the Great was an Idumaean named king of Judea by the Roman Senate in 40 BCE, who took Jerusalem from the last Hasmonean king in 37 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodias"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herodias.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herodias</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herodias was a Herodian princess, a granddaughter of Herod the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodium"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herodium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herodium</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Herodium is a fortified palace built by Herod the Great on an artificially heightened, cone-shaped hill south of Bethlehem, in the West Bank.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herodotus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herodotus was a Greek historian of the fifth century BCE, often called the father of history, whose Histories describe the peoples of the Persian world and the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/herophilus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/herophilus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Herophilus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Herophilus of Chalcedon was a Greek physician who worked in Alexandria under the early Ptolemies and is often called the father of anatomy.</span></a></li></ul>
